15. MANUSCRIPT-SAFE MODEL DESCRIPTION
======================================================================

The computational model consisted of a patient-specific ventricular
solid domain reconstructed from end-diastolic/end-systolic cardiac
segmentations. The ventricular tissue was represented using a nonlinear
anisotropic hyperelastic constitutive formulation of the
Holzapfel–Ogden type with a nearly incompressible treatment. Myocardial
fiber orientation was assigned using a rule/atlas-based transmural
helical architecture, with prescribed endocardial and epicardial helix
angles of +60° and −60°, respectively. A prescribed end-diastolic cavity
loading condition and a viscoelastic spring–damper basal constraint were
used to define the mechanical boundary conditions. Gravity-dependent
loading was introduced through an external body-force multiplier and
evaluated at 1.00, 0.75, 0.50, 0.38, 0.16, and 0.00 g. Three-dimensional
tetrahedral meshes were assessed using a coarse-to-fine convergence
strategy, and the nonlinear finite-element equations were solved using
a fully coupled Newton method. Primary mechanical outputs were peak
von Mises stress, peak principal strain, maximum ventricular
displacement, LV cavity volume, myocardial volume, and strain energy.

This description is a methodological specification. Numerical parameter
values not independently recovered from the final simulation-input
artifact or native COMSOL model must not be added solely to complete the
description.

